    "case_id": "mxh046",
    "case_summary": "A 4-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for a routine well-child checkup. He began walking at 20 months of age. He is able to drink from a cup but cannot use silverware. He speaks in 2-word phrases and can stack 4 blocks. He can scribble but is unable to draw a circle. His height is above the 99th percentile, and his weight is at the 15th percentile. Vital signs are normal. On examination, he has bilateral inferior lens dislocation, long slender fingers, a high-arched palate, and hyperelastic skin. When he wraps one hand around the opposite wrist, his thumb and fifth finger overlap. What is the most likely cause of these findings?\n\nOptions:\nA. Fibrillin 1 deficiency\nB. Procollagen peptidase deficiency\nC. Type I collagen mutation\nD. Deficiency of pyridoxine-dependent enzymes\nE. Alpha-1,4-glucosidase deficiency\nF. Mutation in the FGFR3 gene\nG. Type V collagen deficiency\nH. Lysyl hydroxylase deficiency\nI. Homogentisic acid oxidase deficiency\nJ. Cystathionine synthase deficiency\n",
